Kent Paul Dolan <well!xanthian@lll-winken.llnl.gov> mailed to me:
 A few requests:

 1) For those of us running Fat Ami's, could you provide a separate
 binary with the whole of
 nethack turned on?  I'd really enjoy pulling up a
 1.7Mbyte nethack in my 9Mbyte Amiga and hacking away.

Well, NetHack is not so large as you think. The load file I now have
is about 730K long, and that is with debugging symbols included.
(Removing them does not save as much as I thought, only about
20-30K at most). And this is with ALL options enabled (which apply
to the Amiga, of course).

 2) Alternately (and probably lots more work) could you overlay it so
 everyone can play the big game?  Admittedly, this might not be a lot of
 fun from floppies unless it is very carefully thought out.

Ick! I hope I don't need to do that! I never used the Manx overlay
manager before, and it will indeed be slow if the overlays are
not carefully separated.

 3) If the folks at the Software Distillary would cooperate, and your
 team has the time, having a PD graphics nethack like John Toebes, VIII's
 Hack v1.0.1.e to give away with the machine would really help sales.
 Graphic hack is a lot more fun than character hack, but nethack is a lot
 more fun than hack, so both would be improvements for the Amiga.

Well personally, I started with Rogue on an ADM-3a terminal, and quite
frankly, I think that is the way 'it has to be'. I really hate those
graphic monsters. I really prefer to see a A instead of some
unrecognizable 8x8 graphics thingy. The hack.font is, in fact, a
compromise, since I only like the wall characters. Buf feel free to
use fed, of course. (In case you'd like to know... I prefer an
ADM-3a over a VT-100 most of the time, simply because it has MUCH
faster screen output! That is of course because the thing is
almost as dumb as a hardcopy terminal)

About giving NetHack away with the machine... it would be a great
idea. But it would mean an extra diskette that goes with the system,
since it would mostly fill the Extras disk by itself!

And about "my team"... well, that consists of exactly one member. Me.
And I have other things to do as well, such as college that is starting
again. And trying to commercialize my messydos file system.

 4) I'm facing a (very long term) project of converting nethack 3.0 to
 C++.  Although Bjarne Stroustrup intended C++ to be a wholly upward
 compatible version of C, Lattice/SAS chose not to distribute their
 C header file kit with their C++, so most C programs won't compile under
 their C++.  Since I've given up using C unless forced (I use it to earn
 a living, for example), if I'm to get nethack 3.0 going on the Amiga at
 Casa Xanth, C++ is it.  With this long an intro, what I'd like to ask is
 that you publish Amiga nethack bug reports (not the fixes, just the "here
 is another bug to watch for") stuff as you go along, not waiting for
 patch releases, just to keep the rest of us busy reinventing the wheel
 informed.

Well, I intended to prepare a diff set for today, of what I did to
patchlevel-1 files to get them to compile again, but it was too late
in the night...
Instead, I'll mention a few points I remember:

Watch out for things they changed behind my back. That is, declarations
for index() and rindex() seem to have been misplaced, and somebody
suddenly introduced off_t, which I needed to make up. I put that one
in stdio.h. Beware of files that include both hack.h and stdio.h, at
least if you use a precompiled/preincluded header file. Hack.h
includes stdio.h already. In lev_comp.c, lines 1010 and 1020 (or so)
have pointers cast to ints, change those to long. (Stupid all-the-
world-is-a-vax-ism). My #define for memcpy (or something) disappeared,
I put a replacement function in amidos.c. And MAXPCHARS is too small,
again! Should be at least 32.
They changed the way the terminal input function is called; fortunately
this leads to an undefined reference, or else a terrible loop would
have resulted. Look in a corresponding file for some other system how
it is supposed to work. (tgetch() should not handle redo-code).

The Manx compiler also found a few (obvious) mistakes, such as a
pointer-valued function that returns false. Manx does not want to
cast chars into pointers, even if they are constant zeros.

I'll start working on patchlevel 2, see what they want me to do with
the alt key, and what other contortions they have though of.

 well!xanthian
 Kent, the man from xanth, now just another echo from The Well.

